{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Like a muddied spring and a corrupted source, so is a righteous man tottering before a wicked man. To eat much honey is not good, and to search out their glory is glory</b> (Mishlei 25:27)." "<b>King Solomon,</b> of blessed memory, informs us here (Mishlei 25) of the virtue of the righteous man. For when the righteous man is delivered and falls into the hand of the wicked man and totters before him, that tottering is only for a moment, not forever, and he is destined to be saved from his hand immediately, as it is written (Mishlei 24:16): ״כי שבע יפול צדיק וקם״ — for a righteous man falls seven times and rises. And in the end he returns to his station and to his glory. And to what is he compared? To a spring, which people tread upon and trample with the foot, and it becomes muddied for a moment; yet that muddying is not forever and has no permanence, for it is destined to return to its clarity. So too the righteous man: the wicked man cannot diminish his glory nor extinguish the light of his station's countenance so that he does not remain in his righteousness as he was — just as no man can muddy the waters of a spring so that they will not afterward return to their clarity and purity as they were. And the reason he likened him to two things, to a spring and to a source: know that a river draws from the spring and the spring draws from the source, and just as the river receives from the spring, so the spring receives from the source. And on this account the spring (מעין) is called by the language of 'eye' (עין), and it is written ״עין המים״ — the spring of waters — for the eye receives from the tears that are drawn from the brain, and therefore one who is constant in tears dries up his brain. But the source does not receive from another, for its waters emerge of themselves and from their own place they multiply and well up and never cease forever. And on this account the Holy One, blessed be He, is called a 'source,' because His power never ceases forever and for all eternity, as it is written (Jeremiah 17:13): ״מקור מים חיים את ה׳״ — the source of living waters is HaShem; and it is written (Psalms 36:10): ״כי עמך מקור חיים״ — for with You is the source of life. And therefore Solomon, of blessed memory, likened the righteous man who totters before a wicked man to a spring and a source, for the muddying of the spring and the corruption of the source are not forever — for who can corrupt a welling source that never ceases?" "<b>And after</b> he informed us in this verse that it is impossible to diminish the glory of the righteous man, just as it is impossible to muddy clear waters so that they will not return to their clarity, he informed us in the second verse that a person is obligated to investigate the righteous and to recount the praise of their virtue, and he tells you that the value of this investigation, in searching out their glory, is better than eating honey. For eating much honey is not good, but multiplying the investigation of their glory is good and is glory before the mind. For there is no doubt that one who continually praises the righteous does so only from the side of his good nature and temperament, and therefore recounting their praise is sweet to him, sweeter than honey. And one who praises the wicked does so only out of evil of heart, and because he has affinity with one of their bad traits, and therefore recounting their praise is sweetened before him, in the manner of what is written (Job 20:12): ״אם תמתיק בפיו רעה״ — though evil be sweet in his mouth; and it is written (Mishlei 28:4): ״עוזבי תורה יהללו רשע״ — those who forsake the Torah praise the wicked. For each and every one praises his own kind." "<b>And therefore</b> King Solomon, of blessed memory, warned that to search out the glory of the righteous and to recount their praise and virtue is itself glory to the one who praises, and whoever is zealous in this matter is praiseworthy, and the multiplication of investigation into their glory is better than eating honey. And so David, of blessed memory, said (Psalms 15:4): ״ואת יראי ה׳ יכבד״ — and he honors those who fear HaShem. He warned to search out the glory of the righteous, and to recount the glory of those who fear HaShem, and to honor them. And this trait he placed among the traits of the devout (hasidim), who ascend the loftiest rungs, who dwell on His holy mountain, as he said (Psalms 15:1–2): ״ה׳ מי יגור באהלך מי ישכון בהר קדשך הולך תמים״ and so forth — HaShem, who shall sojourn in Your tent, who shall dwell on Your holy mountain? He who walks blamelessly — and afterward (v. 4): ״נבזה בעיניו נמאס ואת יראי ה׳ יכבד״ — a despicable person is despised in his eyes, but he honors those who fear HaShem." "<b>And already</b> our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded ״ואת יראי ה׳ יכבד״ — this is Jehoshaphat king of Judah, who when he saw a Torah scholar would rise from his throne and embrace him and kiss him and call him 'my rabbi and my teacher.'" "<b>And one should wonder</b> how the Rabbis, of blessed memory, included among these upper attributes what Jehoshaphat practiced — things that the rest of the multitudes are obligated and do likewise. And what is the distinction of Jehoshaphat over all the rest of Israel? It is like the matter that the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Ezekiel 4:14): ״נבלה וטרפה לא אכלתי״ — and what was the distinction of Ezekiel? But the intention regarding Jehoshaphat is that he was a king and was not obligated in this, for it is written (Deuteronomy 17:15): ״שום תשים עליך מלך״ — that his fear should be upon you; and even so, he practiced this, and that is the distinction." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> ״A failing spring and a ruined cistern, a righteous man weakening before a wicked one״ — this is Jacob, who called Esau 'my lord' many times. End quote. And although in the end he was saved from his hand and returned to his clarity like a spring, nevertheless we find that he collapsed and humbled himself before him to a great extent, for he needed to send him a gift and sent him angels — as it is written." "<b>And Jacob sent angels before him to Esau his brother, to the land of Seir, the field of Edom.</b> The word 'angels' (מלאכים) embraces both human messengers and the ministering angels dispatched by the Holy One, blessed be He. And since Jacob needed to go to Paddan-aram because of the fear of Esau his brother, therefore now upon returning to his land he sought to appease his brother's wrath and inquire after his welfare, and sent his messengers to him — his servants and the people of his household. And he sent with them some of the angels who had encountered him, in the manner written in the preceding parashah (Genesis 32:2): ״ויפגעו בו מלאכי אלהים.״ And this is like the practice of Abraham his grandfather, who prayed to the Name, may He be exalted, that He send the angel with the servant, as he said (Genesis 24:7): ״ישלח מלאכו לפניך.״ And I have already explained in the parashah of ויהיו חיי שרה that the angel preceded the servant to the water-spring. And this is what is said here: ״וישלח יעקב מלאכים לפניו״ — for the angels who encountered him on the road and came to guard him went with his messengers and preceded him to Esau his brother, so that he would not be able to harm him. For the word מלאכים bears two categories of emissaries: physical and intellectual. And what strengthens this somewhat is his saying ״וישובו המלאכים״ — and if they had been only human messengers, it would have been fitting to say ויבאו המלאכים אל עשו, and they told him such and such, and they returned to Jacob, saying: we have told him these things. But this formulation indicates that they were ministering angels, and therefore he said ״וישובו,״ for they returned before he went; and afterward he said ״באנו אל אחיך אל עשו״ — these are human beings, and therefore he said ״באנו.״" "<b>And it is possible</b> to say, in the secret of the matter, that the messengers were both men and angels, like the matter said regarding Abraham. And therefore Scripture brought forth the messengers in the language of 'angels' (מלאכים), and all of it is truth — outer and inner." "<b>And it appears</b> that this is also the view of the sage Rabbi Abraham, from what he wrote in the parashah of וירא regarding Balaam: ״and the she-ass spoke״ — and if you understand the secret of the angels of Abraham and also of Jacob, then you will understand the truth. End quote." "<b>And you should know</b> that Yitzhak his father was dwelling in the land of the Negev, and Yaakov had to pass through the land of Edom; therefore he feared Esav, lest he come out to meet him, and therefore he sent messengers ahead to him in his land." "<b>And our Rabbis, of blessed memory,</b> took hold of it from what they expounded in Bereshit Rabbah: (Proverbs 26:17) ״מחזיק באזני כלב עובר מתעבר על ריב לא לו״ — one who seizes a dog by the ears passes by and is caught up in a quarrel not his own. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: you were going on your way and you were sending to him and saying, ״כה אמר עבדך יעקב״ — thus says your servant Yaakov; this is what the verse says: (Proverbs 25:26) ״מעין נרפש ומקור משחת צדיק מט לפני רשע״ — a muddied spring and a corrupted well, a righteous man bowing down before a wicked man. Yaakov called Esav 'my master'; the Torah teaches derech eretz, to give honor to the sovereign. Rabbenu HaKadosh would write to King Antoninus: ״כה אמר עבדך יהודה״ — thus says your servant Yehudah. Antoninus sent to him: are you my servant? Would that I were your servant in the World to Come! He said to him: I am not greater than my forefather, nor are you lesser than your forefather, for Yaakov my forefather sent to Esav, ״כה אמר עבדך יעקב״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {9 "The citation 'והישר כי האתון דברה' appears corrupt or truncated; rendered hyper-literally. The reference to Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra) and Balaam's ass in the parashah of וירא is textually difficult to parse with certainty.", 11 "Hebrew text cites Proverbs 26:17 parenthetically immediately after 'בב״ר' (Bereshit Rabbah); the prooftext from Proverbs 25:26 appears mid-comment without a separate citation. Both rendered as found."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:17:35.256470940Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}